Human–Robot Operations Engineer (HROE): Theoretical Foundations, Academic Roadmap, and Research Agenda for an Emerging Interdisciplinary Profession

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

## ABSTRACT

The accelerating deployment of autonomous robotic systems in shared human workspaces marks one of the most consequential technological transitions of the twenty-first century. Total deal value in robotics — spanning venture financing, mergers and acquisitions, equity offerings, and partnerships — surged to $7.3 billion in the first half of 2025 alone, while robots are rapidly diversifying from caged assembly lines into collaborative, mobile, and humanoid systems across healthcare, construction, and logistics. [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2025/11/06/3182255/0/en/Industrial-Robots-Research-Report-2025-Humanoid-AI-Driven-and-Collaborative-Robotics-Set-New-Benchmark-for-Adaptive-Flexible-and-Smart-Manufacturing-Automation.html) Yet the engineering discipline responsible for safely and efficiently coordinating humans and robots within these shared physical environments remains theoretically underdeveloped and institutionally unnamed. This dissertation prospectus introduces and formally defines the **Human–Robot Operations Engineer (HROE)** as a coherent professional and academic identity, establishes its mathematical and theoretical foundations, and proposes a structured Ph.D. curriculum and four-year research agenda. The document argues that the dominant bottleneck in physical automation is no longer robotic capability, but rather the design and optimization of the human–robot operational interface at scale.

