## PART I: INTRODUCTION AND MOTIVATION

### 1.1 The Emergence of a New Operational Layer

The industrial history of automation has proceeded in recognizable phases. The first phase mechanized physical labor. The second phase computerized information processing. The third — currently underway — introduces autonomous mobile and collaborative robots into environments that are fundamentally designed for, and continuously occupied by, human beings.

One of the most important trends reshaping sectors from manufacturing to construction is the emergence of human–robot collaboration (HRC), which holds the potential to redefine traditional standards of project execution and operational management. [Springer](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-97697-1_33) The term "collaboration" was introduced specifically to emphasize genuine interaction between humans and machines while simultaneously restoring the prominence of human factors in system design — a recognition that purely technical optimization is insufficient. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S016981412500143X)

This transition creates a structural gap in the engineering profession. Mechanical engineers design the robots. Software engineers program them. Control engineers stabilize them. Yet no established discipline owns the problem of coordinating hundreds or thousands of robots alongside human workers within a living, dynamic, safety-critical workspace. The **Human–Robot Operations Engineer** is the professional definition that fills this gap.

### 1.2 Statement of the Problem

The core problem can be stated precisely. Given a shared spatio-temporal workspace $\mathcal{W} \subset \mathbb{R}^3 \times \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$, populated by a set of human agents $\mathcal{H} = \{H_1, H_2, \ldots, H_m\}$ and a fleet of robotic agents $\mathcal{R} = \{R_1, R_2, \ldots, R_n\}$, each with their own dynamic configurations, task graphs, and behavioral uncertainties, the HROE must design, operate, and continuously optimize the system so that:

$$\min_{\pi \in \Pi} \; J(\pi) = \alpha \cdot T_{\text{makespan}} + \beta \cdot C_{\text{discomfort}} + \gamma \cdot P_{\text{risk}} + \delta \cdot E_{\text{fatigue}}$$

subject to:

$$d\bigl(q_H(t),\, q_R(t)\bigr) \;\geq\; d_{\min} \quad \forall\, H \in \mathcal{H},\; R \in \mathcal{R},\; t \geq 0$$

$$\sum_{r \in \mathcal{R}} u_r(t) \;\leq\; U_{\max}$$

$$\phi_H(t) \;\leq\; \phi_{\max}, \quad \forall\, H \in \mathcal{H}$$

where $\pi$ is the joint operational policy, $q_H(t)$ and $q_R(t)$ are the human and robot configurations at time $t$, $d_{\min}$ is the minimum safety separation distance, $u_r(t)$ is robot utilization, and $\phi_H(t)$ is the cumulative fatigue index for each human worker. The scalar weights $\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta \geq 0$ encode the operational trade-off priorities of the deploying organization.

This is not a problem of robotics alone, nor of industrial engineering alone, nor of human factors alone. It is intrinsically interdisciplinary, and it is NP-hard in its general formulation.

### 1.3 Why a New Ph.D. is Required

Developing solutions that are both technologically sophisticated and human-centered requires a holistic approach — one that encompasses the social and psychological effects of incorporating robots into human-centered work environments alongside the complexities of design and implementation. Research on human–robot interaction will be crucial for enhancing the operator's work conditions and wellbeing as well as production performance. [Frontiers](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/robotics-and-ai/articles/10.3389/frobt.2025.1564948/full) No existing doctoral program synthesizes all of the required competencies — control theory, operations research, human factors engineering, probabilistic inference, and safety-critical systems — into a single coherent research identity.

