## PART III: MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATIONS

### 3.1 Safety Constraints via Control Barrier Functions

The most rigorous modern framework for safety in dynamic human–robot environments is the **Control Barrier Function (CBF)** methodology. Control Barrier Functions can be conceptualized as mathematical tools that constrain the control actions of a dynamical system, enabling forward invariance for a desired set of states. This desired state set can either keep the robot away from obstacles or compel the system to exhibit specific behaviors. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0967066125003867)

Formally, define a safety set $\mathcal{C} \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ as:

$$\mathcal{C} = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n \mid h(x) \geq 0\}$$

A continuously differentiable function $h: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$ is a valid CBF if there exists a class $\mathcal{K}$ function $\alpha$ such that:

$$\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \dot{h}(x, u) \right] \geq -\alpha(h(x)), \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{C}$$

This guarantees that the robot controller can always find an admissible control input $u$ that keeps the system within $\mathcal{C}$. The resulting safety filter modifies a nominal control policy $u_{\text{nom}}$ minimally:

$$u^* = \arg\min_{u} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2 \quad \text{subject to} \quad \dot{h}(x,u) + \alpha(h(x)) \geq 0$$

Recent work combines CBFs with conformal risk control to provide formal safety guarantees while accounting for complex human behavior, using conformal risk control to quantify and bound prediction errors in CBF safety values and establishing formal probabilistic guarantees on constraint satisfaction during interaction. [University of Washington](https://depts.washington.edu/ctrl/publications/)

A key advance incorporates human–robot relative velocity directly into the CBF formulation, coupling the kinematics of mobile platforms and robotic arms to address threats from fast relative motion between humans and robots in environments of dense human–robot coexistence. [nih](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12251740/)

### 3.2 Hierarchical Safety Prioritization

Real human bodies are not uniform safety objects. A hierarchical CBF design prioritizes enhanced safety for more vulnerable anatomical regions, assigning higher safety margins to the head than to the hand, for example, and implementing proactive collision risk mitigation in close-proximity interaction scenarios through real-time 3D human pose detection. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0967066125003867)

The hierarchical constraint structure can be written as:

$$h_k(x) \geq 0, \quad k = 1, 2, \ldots, K, \quad \text{with priority ordering} \quad p_1 \succ p_2 \succ \cdots \succ p_K$$

solved via a cascaded quadratic program:

$$u^*_k = \arg\min_{u} \|u - u^*_{k-1}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \dot{h}_k + \alpha_k(h_k) \geq 0$$

### 3.3 Hybrid Scheduling as a Constrained Optimization Problem

The task allocation problem across a mixed human–robot workforce is formalized as a **hybrid job-shop scheduling problem**. Let $\mathcal{T} = \{T_1, T_2, \ldots, T_N\}$ be the task graph with precedence constraints $T_i \prec T_j$. Human processors $H_i$ are modeled as **stochastic processors** with processing time distributions $\tau_{H_i}(T_k) \sim \mathcal{D}(\mu_k, \sigma_k)$. Robotic processors $R_j$ are modeled as **deterministic processors** with known processing times $\tau_{R_j}(T_k) = c_{jk}$.

The scheduling objective is:

$$\min_{A \in \{0,1\}^{(m+n) \times N}} \; C_{\max}(A)$$

subject to:

$$\sum_{i} A_{iT_k} = 1 \quad \forall T_k \in \mathcal{T} \quad \text{(each task assigned exactly once)}$$

$$A_{iT_j} = 0 \;\text{ if }\; T_i \prec T_j \;\text{ and }\; \text{finish}(T_i) > \text{start}(T_j) \quad \text{(precedence)}$$

$$\phi_{H_i}(t) = \sum_{T_k: A_{iT_k}=1} w(T_k, t) \leq \phi_{\max} \quad \text{(fatigue)}$$

Recent work in the Industry 5.0 context has addressed the inadequacy of static and centralized task allocation methods, developing distributed multi-robot dynamic task allocation approaches for digital twin factories that employ the Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers and Augmented Lagrangian Coordination to derive high-quality, scalable solutions within decentralized frameworks. [Taylor & Francis Online](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00207543.2025.2499866)

### 3.4 Human Intention Prediction via Probabilistic Inference

The HROE system must predict human actions before they occur, not merely respond to them. This requires formalizing human behavior as a latent-variable stochastic process.

**Bayesian Intention Inference.** Let $\mathcal{G} = \{g_1, g_2, \ldots, g_K\}$ be the set of possible human goals. Given observed trajectory $\xi_{0:t}$, the posterior over goals is:

$$P(g_k \mid \xi_{0:t}) \propto P(\xi_{0:t} \mid g_k) \cdot P(g_k)$$

**POMDP Formulation.** The robot's decision problem in the presence of uncertain human intent is formalized as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process:

$$\langle \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{O}, T, R, Z, \gamma \rangle$$

where $\mathcal{S}$ is the joint state space of human and robot, $\mathcal{A}$ is the robot's action space, $\mathcal{O}$ is the observation space, $T$ is the transition model, $R$ the reward function, $Z$ the observation model, and $\gamma$ the discount factor. Existing POMDP-based models address uncertainty using belief distributions over latent states, and current approaches to human-AI collaboration focus on identifying human intent — either probabilistically or deterministically — and acting to complement it for coordination. [Umass](https://rlj.cs.umass.edu/2025/papers/RLJ_RLC_2025_149.pdf)

**Inverse Reinforcement Learning for Goal Inference.** Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) addresses the challenge of manually defining the reward function in Markov Decision Processes, which can result in sub-optimal policies, by instead inferring the reward function from observed human behavior — enabling robots to learn human preferences and predict future actions from demonstrated trajectories. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2218-6581/14/12/174)

The IRL objective is:

$$\hat{R} = \arg\max_{R} \; \mathbb{E}_{\pi^*_R}\left[\sum_t R(s_t, a_t)\right] - \mathbb{E}_{\pi^H}\left[\sum_t R(s_t, a_t)\right]$$

where $\pi^H$ is the observed human policy and $\pi^*_R$ is the optimal policy under $R$.

Empirical results confirm that IRL-based prediction systems outperform competing methods, with users feeling comfortable initiating handover at 60% of total task distance when the robot achieves 90% target prediction accuracy, reaching 99.9% accuracy when less than 20% of the task remains. [IEEE Xplore](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10610595/)

### 3.5 Human-Aware Trajectory Planning

Classical trajectory planning minimizes path length. Human-aware trajectory planning minimizes a composite cost that explicitly accounts for human psychological and safety factors:

$$\mathcal{J}_{\text{HATP}}(\xi) = \underbrace{w_1 \cdot \ell(\xi)}_{\text{Path Length}} + \underbrace{w_2 \cdot \int_0^T \mathcal{D}(\xi(t), \mathcal{H}(t))\,dt}_{\text{Discomfort}} + \underbrace{w_3 \cdot \int_0^T \mathcal{V}(\xi(t), \dot{\xi}(t))\,dt}_{\text{Visibility/Legibility}} + \underbrace{w_4 \cdot P_{\text{collision}}(\xi \mid \hat{\mathcal{H}})}_{\text{Risk}}$$

where $\mathcal{D}$ is a discomfort field based on proxemics theory, $\mathcal{V}$ is a legibility penalty encouraging predictable robot motion, and $P_{\text{collision}}$ is the collision probability under the predicted human trajectory distribution $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$.

### 3.6 Digital Twin Operational Framework

Digital twins in human–robot collaboration serve three primary functions: optimizing production workflows through real-time simulation and analysis, leveraging real-time data for dynamic task allocation to increase system flexibility, and improving worker safety by intelligently managing the interaction environment. [DrPress](https://drpress.org/ojs/index.php/HSET/article/view/21587)

The digital twin state is defined as:

$$\Sigma_{\text{DT}}(t) = \left\langle \hat{q}_H^{(1:m)}(t),\; q_R^{(1:n)}(t),\; \hat{\mathcal{T}}(t),\; \hat{\phi}^{(1:m)}(t),\; \hat{\mathcal{E}}(t+\Delta) \right\rangle$$

where $\hat{q}_H^{(1:m)}(t)$ are estimated human configurations, $q_R^{(1:n)}(t)$ are robot configurations, $\hat{\mathcal{T}}(t)$ is the current task graph state, $\hat{\phi}^{(1:m)}(t)$ are fatigue estimates, and $\hat{\mathcal{E}}(t + \Delta)$ is the predicted event distribution over the lookahead horizon $\Delta$.

The most advanced implementations couple digital twin-driven robot models with task dynamic allocation processes to facilitate real-time monitoring and anomaly resolution, enabling the virtual-physical mapping and predictive analytics required for adaptive operation at the production execution level. [ResearchGate](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/391460029_Distributed_multi-robot_task_dynamic_allocation_in_digital-twin_factory_towards_industry_50)

