## PART VI: FOUR RESEARCH DIRECTIONS — DETAILED PROPOSALS

### Research Direction 1: Safety-Guaranteed Collaboration Under Probabilistic Human Uncertainty

**Motivation.** Classical CBF guarantees assume deterministic knowledge of human position. Real deployments involve noisy pose estimation, partial occlusion, and genuinely uncertain human intent.

**Problem Formulation.** Let $\hat{q}_H(t) = q_H(t) + \epsilon(t)$ where $\epsilon(t) \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma_t)$ is observation noise with time-varying covariance. The goal is to design a **probabilistic CBF** such that:

$$P\left(h(x(t)) \geq 0 \;\forall\; t \geq 0\right) \geq 1 - \delta$$

for a specified risk tolerance $\delta \in (0, 1)$.

This class of approaches uses conformal risk control to quantify and bound prediction errors in CBF safety values, establishing formal guarantees on the probability of constraint satisfaction during interaction — enabling robots to maintain provable safety margins even when human motion predictions are noisy and probabilistic. [University of Washington](https://depts.washington.edu/ctrl/publications/)

**Expected Contribution:** A complete theoretical framework for uncertainty-aware CBF design with formal probability bounds, validated on a 7-DOF robotic arm operating alongside a human assembly worker.

---

### Research Direction 2: Multi-Robot Fleet Coordination Around Human Teams

**Motivation.** Most CBF and HRC literature addresses a single robot and a single human. Real warehouse and manufacturing deployments involve dozens of autonomous mobile robots and groups of workers simultaneously occupying overlapping zones.

**Problem Formulation.** For a fleet $\mathcal{R} = \{R_1, \ldots, R_n\}$ and human team $\mathcal{H} = \{H_1, \ldots, H_m\}$, the multi-robot safety problem requires:

$$d(q_{R_i}(t), q_{R_j}(t)) \geq d_{\min}^{RR}, \quad \forall i \neq j$$
$$d(q_{R_i}(t), q_{H_k}(t)) \geq d_{\min}^{RH}, \quad \forall i, k$$

simultaneously, under a decentralized control architecture where no robot has global state knowledge.

Investigations across vehicle assembly, warehouse logistics, and agricultural operations — drawing on expert perspectives from 31 technical specialists across nine European countries — confirm that safety, intuitive interfaces, and trust are the central unresolved challenges in scaling collaborative robotics across industrial sectors. [Frontiers](https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/robotics-and-ai/articles/10.3389/frobt.2024.1342130/full)

**Expected Contribution:** A distributed CBF-based coordination protocol for mixed human–robot fleets, with computational complexity analysis and real-world validation in a warehouse testbed.

---

### Research Direction 3: Ergonomic Load Optimization via Proactive Task Reallocation

**Motivation.** Human workers in human–robot teams face a paradox: robots can relieve physical load but also create novel ergonomic risks through awkward postures, forced pacing, and cognitive overload.

**Problem Formulation.** Define a cumulative ergonomic load index:

$$\Phi_H(T) = \int_0^T \left[ w_{\text{phys}} \cdot L_{\text{physical}}(H, t) + w_{\text{cog}} \cdot L_{\text{cognitive}}(H, t) \right] dt$$

The HROE must dynamically reallocate tasks such that $\Phi_H(T) \leq \Phi_{\max}$ for all workers, using real-time physiological monitoring (heart rate variability, EMG, gaze metrics).

Over-assistive robots can adversely impact long-term human-robot collaboration, leading to risks of worker complacency, reduced workforce skill sets, and diminished situational awareness — meaning that ergonomics practitioners must look beyond merely optimizing user trust and comfort and instead introduce variability and adaptation into robot behavior to prevent these negative consequences. [Taylor & Francis Online](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/24725838.2024.2329114)

**Expected Contribution:** A closed-loop ergonomic monitoring and task reallocation system integrating wearable biosensors, CBF-constrained robot controllers, and a fatigue prediction model validated over multi-hour work sessions.

---

### Research Direction 4: Large-Scale Digital Twin Operations for Human–Robot Factories

**Motivation.** As factories deploy hundreds of robots alongside hundreds of workers, no human operator can maintain situational awareness at the required resolution. A real-time digital twin that simulates the next 15–60 minutes of operations enables proactive intervention before bottlenecks and safety violations emerge.

**System Architecture.** The proposed HROE Digital Twin (HROE-DT) system comprises five layers:

1. **Sensor Layer:** Computer vision, LiDAR arrays, wearable IMUs, force-torque feedback
2. **State Estimation Layer:** Multi-target tracking with Kalman/particle filters
3. **Prediction Layer:** POMDP + IRL-based human behavior prediction
4. **Optimization Layer:** Distributed MILP task reallocation engine
5. **Intervention Layer:** Automated alerts + CBF safety corrections

The most advanced multi-robot task allocation frameworks now integrate digital twins, integer programming, and large language models, enabling narrative-driven schedule adaptation in which unstructured natural language inputs are interpreted by an LLM and optimization constraints are autonomously updated — with top-performing models achieving over 97% accuracy in constraint and parameter extraction. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.18178)

**Expected Contribution:** A complete HROE-DT architecture demonstrated on a large-scale simulation (50+ robots, 30+ human workers), with quantitative evaluation of safety incident reduction, throughput improvement, and ergonomic load variance.

