The Human–Robot Operations Engineer stands at the precise intersection where this vision must be operationalized. If the twentieth century produced the industrial engineer who coordinated human labor and machines, the twenty-first century requires the HROE who coordinates human intelligence and robotic automation in shared physical environments. The mathematics is harder, the safety stakes are higher, and the potential impact is correspondingly greater.

APPENDIX A: CONTROL BARRIER FUNCTIONS — FORMAL DERIVATION AND PROOFS

A.1 Continuous-Time Control Barrier Functions

Consider a control-affine dynamical system

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u, \quad x \in \mathcal{D} \subset \mathbb{R}^n, \ u \in \mathcal{U} \subset \mathbb{R}^m,

with f and g locally Lipschitz. Let a closed set \mathcal{C} \subset \mathcal{D} be the safe set, defined as the zero-superlevel set of a continuously differentiable function h:\mathcal{D}\to\mathbb{R}:

\mathcal{C} = \{x \in \mathcal{D} \mid h(x) \ge 0\}, \quad \partial\mathcal{C} = \{x \in \mathcal{D} \mid h(x)=0\}, \quad \mathrm{Int}(\mathcal{C}) = \{x \in \mathcal{D} \mid h(x) > 0\}.

Definition A.1 (Control Barrier Function).
The function h is a Control Barrier Function (CBF) for system \dot{x}=f(x)+g(x)u on \mathcal{C} if there exists a class \mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\sup_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \bigl[ L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u \bigr] \ge -\alpha(h(x)), \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{C}, \tag{A.1}

where L_f h = \nabla h \cdot f and L_g h = \nabla h \cdot g are the Lie derivatives.

Theorem A.1 (Forward Invariance).
If h is a CBF on \mathcal{C} and u(x) is any locally Lipschitz controller satisfying

L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u(x) + \alpha(h(x)) \ge 0 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{C}, \tag{A.2}

then the set \mathcal{C} is forward invariant: if x(0)\in\mathcal{C}, then x(t)\in\mathcal{C} for all t\ge0.

Proof.
Let x(t) be the solution of the closed-loop system with controller satisfying (A.2). Define y(t)=h(x(t)). Then

\dot{y}(t) = \nabla h(x) \cdot \dot{x} = L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u(x).

Condition (A.2) gives \dot{y}(t) \ge -\alpha(y(t)). By the comparison lemma, since \alpha is class \mathcal{K}, y(t)\ge \eta(t) where \eta solves \dot{\eta} = -\alpha(\eta) with \eta(0)=y(0). Because \alpha(r)>0 for r>0, \eta(t)\ge0 whenever \eta(0)\ge0. Thus y(t)\ge0 for all t, i.e., x(t)\in\mathcal{C}. ∎

CBF-based safety filter (quadratic program).
Given a nominal control u_{\text{nom}}, the minimal intervention controller is

u^* = \arg\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u + \alpha(h(x)) \ge 0. \tag{A.3}

Since the constraint is linear in u and the objective is convex, (A.3) is a convex quadratic program (QP) solvable in real time.

A.2 CBF for Human–Robot Separation

For a robot with state x_R = (p_R, v_R, \ldots) and a human with estimated position p_H, define

h(x) = \|p_R - p_H\|^2 - d_{\min}^2.

The safe set \mathcal{C} = \{x \mid \|p_R-p_H\|\ge d_{\min}\}. Using the robot’s single-integrator or double-integrator model, one can compute L_f h and L_g h to enforce the separation constraint. Recent work incorporates relative velocity into the CBF to handle fast approach (see the main text references). The Lie derivative involves the dot product of the relative position vector and the relative velocity, capturing whether the robot is moving toward the human.

APPENDIX B: PROBABILISTIC SAFETY WITH CONFORMAL RISK CONTROL

B.1 Problem Statement

In practice, the human configuration q_H(t) is estimated with uncertainty: \hat{q}_H(t) = q_H(t) + \epsilon(t), \epsilon(t)\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma_t). A deterministic CBF using \hat{q}_H may fail to guarantee safety with high probability. We seek a probabilistic CBF such that

\mathbb{P}\Bigl( h(x(t)) \ge 0 \ \forall t \ge 0 \Bigr) \ge 1-\delta, \quad \delta\in(0,1). \tag{B.1}

B.2 Conformal Risk Control for Safety Values

Conformal prediction provides distribution-free confidence sets for prediction errors. For a predictor that outputs a safety value \hat{h}(x) (e.g., based on estimated human pose), we can use conformal risk control (CRC) to adjust the threshold so that the true safety condition holds with a desired probability.

Setting.
Let (X_i,Y_i)_{i=1}^{N} be a calibration set where X_i are states and Y_i is the true safety value h(x_i) (or an indicator of violation). A base predictor \hat{f}(x) outputs a predicted safety margin \hat{h}(x). Define a loss function \ell(\lambda; x, y) = 1\{ y < \hat{h}(x) - \lambda \} for a correction \lambda \in \mathbb{R}. For a target risk \delta, CRC selects the smallest \lambda such that the empirical risk on the calibration set is bounded:

\hat{\lambda} = \inf\bigl\{ \lambda : \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \ell(\lambda; X_i, Y_i) \le \delta - \frac{1-\delta}{N} \bigr\}.

Then, for a new i.i.d. test point (X,Y), the corrected safety condition Y \ge \hat{h}(X) - \hat{\lambda} holds with probability at least 1-\delta.

In the CBF context, we predict the future value of h(x(t+\Delta t)) given the belief over human motion, and apply a correction \lambda to the constraint:

\hat{h}(x) + \lambda \ge 0,

so that the real safety bound holds with high probability. This yields the conformal CBF-QP:

u^* = \arg\min \|u - u_{\text{nom}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f \hat{h}(x) + L_g \hat{h}(x) u + \alpha(\hat{h}(x)) \ge \varepsilon,

with \varepsilon tuned by CRC to meet the 1-\delta guarantee.

Formal guarantee (informal statement): Under the i.i.d. assumption and with a sufficiently rich calibration set, the controller ensures \mathbb{P}(\inf_{t\ge 0} h(x(t)) \ge 0) \ge 1-\delta.

B.3 Incorporating Probabilistic Human Motion Prediction

Given a human motion predictor \hat{q}_H(\tau) with covariance \Sigma_\tau, the safety margin h becomes a random variable. One can propagate the uncertainty through the CBF constraint. A simpler approach is to shrink the safe set by a tube proportional to the estimation error:

h_{\text{robust}}(x) = \|p_R - \hat{p}_H\|^2 - (d_{\min} + \kappa \sigma)^2,

where \sigma is the maximal standard deviation of the human position estimate, and \kappa chosen to meet the risk budget \delta (e.g., via Chebyshev or Gaussian quantile). Then the standard CBF on h_{\text{robust}} provides probabilistic safety.

APPENDIX C: HIERARCHICAL SAFETY PRIORITIZATION VIA CASCADED QPs

C.1 Priority-Structured CBFs

Given a set of safety constraints h_1\ge0, h_2\ge0, \dots, h_K\ge0 with strict priority ordering p_1 \succ p_2 \succ \cdots \succ p_K (e.g., head safety over hand safety), the hierarchical CBF controller is constructed as a cascade of QPs. The first QP enforces the highest-priority constraint, the second enforces the next priority while deviating minimally from the first solution, and so on.

Algorithm C.1 (Cascaded Safety QP).
Initialize u_0^* = u_{\text{nom}}.
For k = 1, 2, \dots, K:

u_k^* = \arg\min_{u\in\mathcal{U}} \|u - u_{k-1}^*\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f h_k(x) + L_g h_k(x) u + \alpha_k(h_k(x)) \ge 0. \tag{C.1}

The final control command is u^* = u_K^*.

Theorem C.1 (Priority Respect).
If the constraints are feasible in order of priority, the cascaded QP produces a control that respects all constraints up to the first infeasible one, and never violates a higher-priority constraint to satisfy a lower-priority one.

Proof sketch: Because the objective in step k is to stay as close as possible to u_{k-1}^*, which already satisfies constraints 1,\dots,k-1, the new constraint h_k is added without sacrificing feasibility of higher-priority constraints, provided the intersection of constraint sets is non-empty. If infeasible, the QP at level k returns the projection of u_{k-1}^* onto the set satisfying constraint k, which may violate lower-priority constraints but not higher ones (since they are already fixed in u_{k-1}^*). ∎

APPENDIX D: HYBRID HUMAN–ROBOT SCHEDULING — COMPLEXITY AND ALGORITHMIC DETAILS

D.1 Formal NP-Hardness

The hybrid job-shop scheduling problem (HJSP) with human stochastic processors, robot deterministic processors, precedence constraints, and fatigue limits is NP-hard. Even the deterministic job-shop with two machines is NP-hard, and our problem generalizes it by adding stochasticity, resource constraints (fatigue), and assignment flexibility. A reduction from 3-Partition or Job-Shop can be constructed.

D.2 Mixed-Integer Programming Formulation

We provide a detailed MILP encoding for a simplified deterministic version (ignoring stochasticity for planning, with safety margins added later). Let tasks T_j have processing times p_{ij} on resource i (human or robot). Binary variable x_{ij}=1 if task j is assigned to resource i. Continuous variable s_j start time, c_j = s_j + \sum_i p_{ij} x_{ij} completion time. Precedence T_j \prec T_k encoded as s_k \ge c_j. Fatigue constraints for human i are linearized as \sum_j w_{ij} x_{ij} \le \Phi_{\max} if we use a simple cumulative load; dynamic fatigue requires iterative re-optimization.

The objective is to minimize makespan C_{\max} = \max_j c_j. The resulting MILP can be solved for small instances. For large-scale, distributed algorithms (ADMM) are employed.

D.3 Distributed ADMM for Multi-Robot Task Allocation

The main text references the use of ADMM (Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers) for decentralized task allocation. In the digital twin factory, each robot agent solves a local subproblem with its own assignment variables, and a central coordinator (digital twin) updates coupling constraints (e.g., no task is assigned twice). The augmented Lagrangian is formed, and agents alternate between local updates and dual variable updates. This yields near-optimal solutions with communication efficiency.

APPENDIX E: POMDP FORMULATION AND BELIEF UPDATES

E.1 POMDP for Human–Robot Interaction

The joint human–robot system is modeled as a POMDP with state s = (s_R, s_H), where s_R includes robot pose/velocity, s_H includes human physical state and hidden intent (goal). The robot’s observation o consists of noisy measurements of human position and possibly gaze/EMG. The robot maintains a belief b(s_H), updated via Bayes’ rule:

b'(s_H') = \eta\, Z(o \mid s_R, s_H')\, \sum_{s_H} T(s_H' \mid s_H, a_R)\, b(s_H),

where a_R is the robot action, T models human transition dynamics (including goal-directed motion), and Z is the observation likelihood.

E.2 Solving the POMDP

Exact solutions are intractable for large state spaces. Approximate methods include point-based value iteration (PBVI), Monte Carlo tree search with particle beliefs (POMCP), or QMDP for real-time control. In HROE, the robot must select actions that maximize a reward that encodes task progress, safety, and ergonomic cost (see main text). The POMDP allows the robot to actively gather information about human intent (e.g., through legible motions) and trade off exploration and exploitation.

APPENDIX F: INVERSE REINFORCEMENT LEARNING FOR INTENT INFERENCE

F.1 Maximum Entropy IRL

Given observed human trajectories \xi_i = (s_1, a_1, s_2, a_2, \ldots, s_T) as demonstrations of an expert policy \pi^H, IRL infers a reward function R(s,a) that explains the behavior. Under the maximum entropy principle, the probability of a trajectory is

P(\xi \mid R) = \frac{1}{Z(R)} \exp\!\left( \sum_t R(s_t, a_t) \right),

with Z(R) the partition function. The reward is parameterized as R(s,a) = \theta^\top \phi(s,a), where \phi are features (e.g., distance to goal, velocity alignment). The log-likelihood of observed demonstrations is maximized:

\theta^* = \arg\max_\theta \sum_{\xi} \log P(\xi \mid \theta).

Gradient ascent uses the difference between feature expectations under the expert and the learned policy:

\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{D}|}\sum_{\xi\in\mathcal{D}} \sum_t \phi(s_t,a_t) - \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta}\left[ \sum_t \phi(s_t,a_t) \right].

The expectation is typically approximated by solving the MDP with current \theta to get policy \pi_\theta, often using value iteration.

F.2 Application to Human Motion Prediction

Once the reward function is learned, we can model the human as a rational agent maximizing this reward. Given the current state and goal hypothesis, the robot can simulate the human’s optimal policy to predict future trajectories, using a forward value iteration or MDP planning. Combining this with Bayesian goal inference yields a probabilistic distribution over future human states, which feeds into the CBF constraints.

APPENDIX G: HUMAN-AWARE TRAJECTORY PLANNING COST FUNCTION DETAILS

The composite cost for a robot trajectory \xi: [0,T] \to \mathcal{X} is

\mathcal{J}_{\text{HATP}}(\xi) = w_1 \ell(\xi) + w_2 \int_0^T \mathcal{D}(\xi(t), \mathcal{H}(t)) dt + w_3 \int_0^T \mathcal{V}(\xi(t), \dot{\xi}(t)) dt + w_4 P_{\text{collision}}(\xi \mid \hat{\mathcal{H}}).

· Discomfort field \mathcal{D}: Based on proxemics, \mathcal{D}(p, H) = \sum_{i} d_0 \exp\!\bigl(-\frac{\|p - p_{H_i}\|^2}{2\sigma^2}\bigr), penalizing close proximity to any human H_i.
· Legibility penalty \mathcal{V}: A measure of predictability, e.g., \mathcal{V} = \| \dot{\xi} - v_{\text{expected}}\|^2, where v_{\text{expected}} is the velocity the human anticipates based on goal-directed motion. More sophisticated legibility metrics minimize the human’s inference error.
· Collision probability P_{\text{collision}}: Computed by integrating over the predicted human trajectory distribution \hat{\mathcal{H}} (e.g., from POMDP/IRL) the probability that the robot and human occupy the same region. Approximated by Monte Carlo sampling or via chance constraints reformulated as deterministic constraints.

Trajectory optimization can be performed using model predictive control (MPC) with this cost, subject to CBF safety constraints that guarantee hard separation while the probabilistic term handles residual risk from prediction errors.

APPENDIX H: DIGITAL TWIN STATE ESTIMATION AND KALMAN FILTERING

The digital twin maintains real-time estimates of human positions, robot states, and task progress. For each human H_i, we use an extended Kalman filter (EKF) or particle filter to fuse multiple sensors (cameras, wearable IMUs). The state vector includes position, velocity, and possibly joint angles. The motion model incorporates a goal-directed force (from IRL) plus noise:

x_{t+1} = x_t + \Delta t \, v_{\text{goal}}(x_t; g) + w_t, \quad w_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q_t).

Observation model:

y_t = h(x_t) + \nu_t, \quad \nu_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R_t).

The digital twin also tracks the task assignment graph \hat{\mathcal{T}}(t) by monitoring completion events via force-torque sensors or vision. The predicted event distribution \hat{\mathcal{E}}(t+\Delta) is derived from the current schedule and the probabilistic human processing times, using queuing theory or Monte Carlo simulation.

APPENDIX I: ERGONOMIC LOAD INDEX AND DYNAMIC TASK REALLOCATION

I.1 Cumulative Fatigue Model

The physical load L_{\text{physical}} is computed using joint torque estimates and posture risk (e.g., RULA/REBA). For a task T_k assigned to human H, the instantaneous load can be modeled from sensor data. The cumulative ergonomic index:

\Phi_H(t) = \int_0^t \bigl[ w_{\text{phys}} L_{\text{physical}}(H,\tau) + w_{\text{cog}} L_{\text{cognitive}}(H,\tau) \bigr] d\tau.

Cognitive load can be estimated from physiological measures (HRV, pupil dilation) or task difficulty ratings (NASA-TLX). The constraint \Phi_H(t) \le \Phi_{\max} can be enforced by a receding horizon controller: at each decision epoch, solve an optimization that reassigns tasks to robots when a human’s projected fatigue exceeds the threshold.

I.2 Proactive Reallocation Algorithm

Step 1. Continuously monitor physiological signals and estimate current load state.
Step 2. Predict future fatigue using a model \frac{d\Phi}{dt} = f_{\text{load}}(task) - r_{\text{recovery}}(\Phi).
Step 3. At each rescheduling interval, solve a MILP or heuristic to reassign upcoming tasks from overloaded humans to available robots or other humans, respecting precedence.
Step 4. Apply the new assignment and notify the human (via interface) and update robot task queues.

This closed-loop system reduces the risk of ergonomic injury and maintains long-term human performance.

